"""
# f64-returning instance methods must survive use as a binop operand

Regression guard for a JIT/LLVM miscompile that produced SILENT WRONG NUMBERS
(exit 0, no diagnostic). This is what zeroed every cell of `Matrix4.multiply`
in `src/lib/common/render_scene/matrix4.spl`.

## The defect

User functions use a uniform i64 return ABI that carries an f64 as raw
IEEE-754 BITS; consumers bitcast i64->f64. `build_vreg_types` recovers the
static type so `compile_binop` knows to emit that bitcast. Free functions
(`MirInst::Call`) and static methods were stamped from `function_return_types`;
INSTANCE methods (`MirInst::MethodCallStatic`) had only a hard-coded builtin
name whitelist and then an empty catch-all, so a user-defined `-> f64` method
left its dest VReg UNTYPED. Float arithmetic then lowered to
`iadd`/`isub`/`imul`/`sdiv` ON THE BIT PATTERN:

    a.get(0) - 1.0        -> 4613937818241073000.0   (bits(3.0) read as an int)
    a.get(2) * b.get(3)   -> 0.0    (bits(11.0)*bits(101.0) is 0 mod 2^64)
    a.get(0) < 5.0        -> false  (comparison INVERTED)

Fixed by stamping float returns for `MethodCallStatic` in
`src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/codegen/instr/body.rs` (cranelift JIT) and
`src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/codegen/llvm/functions.rs` (LLVM/native).
Same family as the 2026-06-21 f64-call-result fix (07d87555f0e), which stamped
free-function results and missed methods.

## Why this spec spawns a probe instead of asserting inline

**JIT-only. The interpreter was correct throughout.** In-process specs are
structurally blind to JIT-only defects -- `describe`/`it`/`expect` are
interpreter intrinsics with no codegen lowering, and a module with no
`fn main` de-JITs regardless. An inline version of these assertions was
written first and passed 8/8 against a binary whose JIT was provably wrong.

So the real work is in `f64_method_return_binop_jit_probe.spl`, a runnable
program with `fn main` and a verdict line, spawned here under an explicitly
NAMED engine. Sabotage receipt (unfixed vs fixed seed, same probe):

    unfixed / jit        -> F64_METHOD_BINOP_VERDICT: pass=1 fail=10
    unfixed / interpret  -> F64_METHOD_BINOP_VERDICT: pass=11 fail=0
    fixed   / jit        -> F64_METHOD_BINOP_VERDICT: pass=11 fail=0
    fixed   / interpret  -> F64_METHOD_BINOP_VERDICT: pass=11 fail=0

Pattern and adoption notes: doc/07_guide/infra/testing/spec_engine_reach.md
Helper tier (verified by sabotage): src/lib/nogc_sync_mut/spec/engine_probe.spl

Run with: bin/simple test test/01_unit/compiler/codegen/f64_method_return_binop_spec.spl
"""

use std.spec
use std.spec.engine_probe.{engine_stdout, is_known_engine}

val _PROBE = "test/01_unit/compiler/codegen/f64_method_return_binop_jit_probe.spl"
val _PASS = "F64_METHOD_BINOP_VERDICT: pass=11 fail=0"

describe "f64 instance-method results in binop operand position (out of process)":
    """
    As a compiler maintainer, I need the f64 method-return guard pointed at a
    NAMED engine, so that a codegen regression that silently turns float
    arithmetic into integer arithmetic on IEEE bits cannot pass review behind
    a spec that only ever ran on the interpreter.
    """

    it "passes the probe under the interpreter":
        # Control column. The interpreter never had this defect, so this arm
        # failing means the probe or the harness broke, not codegen.
        expect(engine_stdout(_PROBE, "interpret")).to_contain(_PASS)

    it "passes the probe under the cranelift JIT":
        # The arm that carries the weight. Observed RED (pass=1 fail=10)
        # against the unfixed seed, GREEN once MethodCallStatic float returns
        # are stamped in build_vreg_types.
        expect(engine_stdout(_PROBE, "jit")).to_contain(_PASS)

    it "rejects an unrecognised engine name instead of silently using the JIT":
        # SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE falls back to JIT on any unknown value --
        # notably "native", which is NOT a mode -- so an A/B built on an
        # unvalidated name would compare the JIT against itself.
        assert_false(is_known_engine("interp"))
        assert_false(is_known_engine("native"))
        assert_true(is_known_engine("jit"))
        assert_true(is_known_engine("interpret"))
